  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nShowers and thunderstorms fired up in the mountains early this \nafternoon with the help of a weak mid-level short wave trough \nlifting through the area, and perhaps a subtle inverted surface \ntrough. While coverage will remain greatest in and near the \nmountains this afternoon into this evening, there will be at \nleast isolated thunderstorms in the area at large.\n\nWhile thunderstorms will by and large wane after sunset this\nevening, a stronger short wave trough to the west, which will \nabsorb the weaker one as it approaches tonight and Friday, may \nbring thunderstorms into the middle Ohio Valley overnight \ntonight, depending upon how convective complexes associated with\nit evolve. However, with the short wave moving into the area \nFriday, thunderstorms could be ongoing in the morning or \nrefiring by late morning, with a more active afternoon on tap.\n\nRiver valley fog and areas of low clouds are likely again \novernight tonight into Friday morning, most likely and mst\npersistent Friday morning where thunderstorms occur this\nafternoon or evening.\n\nOverall, mid/upper-level troughing/weakness continues over the\neastern third of the U.S. into the weekend, with the associated\nchance for mainly, but not exclusively, diurnal thunderstorms.\n\nThe eastern U.S. trough position moves to or just off the east \ncoast early next week, allowing a west to northwest mid/upper-\nlevel flow to take hold over the forecast area. After a brief \nreprieve in response to this on Sunday as weak surface high \npressure become established, the Ad Nauseam of daily \nthunderstorms returns Monday, or even Sunday night, as the \nsurface high gives way to low level southwest flow beneath the \nmid/upper-level west to northwest flow.\n\nModels are suggesting that troughing along the east coast \namplifies enough to bring a surface cold front through the area,\nwith an uptick in thunderstorms ahead of the front eventually \nfollowed by drier weather in its wake.\n\nThe main threat from thunderstorms continues to be localized \nflooding given relatively slow motion and PW values of 1.5 to 2\ninches. The Weather Prediction Center has the forecast area in \na Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall the next five days, \nextending the streak to at least 7. The orientation shift to \nwest-northwest to east-southeast early next week reflects the \nwest-northwest mid/upper level flow.\n\nWhile low shear for the most part keeps the severe weather \nthreat low through the weekend, increased shear associated with\nthe aforementioned flow pattern at times may increase the \nsevere weather threat in the form of a damaging wind potential \nearly next week. The Storm Prediction Center carries a general \nthunderstorm risk the next 3 days, and has no severe risk for \ndays 4 through 8 next week, although, in their discussion, they \ndo allude to a damaging wind potential associated with possible \nwaves rotating around the large-scale upper trough over Canada \namid the ongoing very moist air mass with possibly strong \ninstability in place.\n\nTemperatures will continue to run slightly above normal this \nweek amid the high humidity.",
